1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,093
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:01,138 --> 00:00:05,616
I will be presenting on building
guardrails for AI-generated frontends,

3
00:00:05,917 --> 00:00:07,945
a platform engineering approach.

4
00:00:08,482 --> 00:00:11,476
Today, I want to take a look
at the problem through a

5
00:00:11,476 --> 00:00:13,016
platform engineering lens.

6
00:00:13,607 --> 00:00:15,266
The core idea is simple.

7
00:00:15,671 --> 00:00:20,277
If we already expect human-written
code to follow design system rules,

8
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:25,165
accessibility requirements, testing
standards, and CI gates, AI-generated

9
00:00:25,205 --> 00:00:27,239
code should not have a separate path.

10
00:00:27,771 --> 00:00:32,828
We should make the good path easier to
follow and the unsafe path harder to ship.

11
00:00:33,917 --> 00:00:37,412
Before we get started, I wanted
to quickly introduce myself.

12
00:00:37,453 --> 00:00:39,650
Hi, my name is Niharika Pujari.

13
00:00:40,004 --> 00:00:43,944
I work across front-end architecture,
accessibility, and software

14
00:00:43,944 --> 00:00:48,261
quality, and I have been thinking
a lot about what changes when AI

15
00:00:48,275 --> 00:00:49,936
starts writing more of our code.

16
00:00:50,350 --> 00:00:56,320
So let's get started AI coding assistants
are very good at compressing the time

17
00:00:56,436 --> 00:00:58,376
between an idea and a block of code.

18
00:00:58,908 --> 00:01:02,542
But that changes the speed of
production more than it changes

19
00:01:02,542 --> 00:01:04,302
the definition of good engineering.

20
00:01:04,916 --> 00:01:09,697
A generated interface may look correct
and still miss keyboard behavior, use

21
00:01:09,707 --> 00:01:14,260
an unapproved component, skip an error
state, or create a testing burden.

22
00:01:14,818 --> 00:01:19,830
The interesting platform question is
not how do we review AI code harder?

23
00:01:20,038 --> 00:01:25,681
It is how do we shape the environment
so that the generated code is naturally

24
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:28,381
lands closer to our engineering standards?

25
00:01:28,892 --> 00:01:30,508
This is another subtle issue.

26
00:01:30,841 --> 00:01:35,862
Reviewers often judge generated code by
how quickly it produces a visible result.

27
00:01:36,426 --> 00:01:40,325
This creates a bias towards
what we can see in a browser.

28
00:01:40,841 --> 00:01:46,589
Platform engineering helps rebalance
that by making invisible requirements

29
00:01:46,649 --> 00:01:53,129
like component actions, automated
checks, and interaction behavior part of

30
00:01:53,131 --> 00:01:56,374
the normal development path Moving on.

31
00:01:56,736 --> 00:01:59,990
Platform engineering also gives
us a useful way to frame this.

32
00:02:00,121 --> 00:02:02,682
A paved path is not just documentation.

33
00:02:02,958 --> 00:02:07,040
It combines good defaults,
reusable building blocks, faster

34
00:02:07,042 --> 00:02:09,296
feedback, and enforceable checks.

35
00:02:09,788 --> 00:02:16,531
For AI-assisted front-end development,
that means the platform is already

36
00:02:16,546 --> 00:02:18,287
carrying more of the standards itself.

37
00:02:18,657 --> 00:02:22,631
The model can still generate
code, but it generates inside an

38
00:02:22,632 --> 00:02:26,298
environment which was approved
already, which was easy to discover.

39
00:02:26,351 --> 00:02:28,763
It had all the expected behaviors visible.

40
00:02:29,134 --> 00:02:34,123
The tests were reusable, and the CI
can reject the known problems already.

41
00:02:34,865 --> 00:02:38,837
That distinction matters because
documentation alone does not

42
00:02:38,852 --> 00:02:40,340
scale with generation speed.

43
00:02:40,603 --> 00:02:44,669
If an engineer has to stop, search
a wiki, interpret a rule, and

44
00:02:44,669 --> 00:02:48,845
remember everything, the platform
is depending a lot on a memory.

45
00:02:49,235 --> 00:02:52,473
The paved path makes the
preferred implementation path

46
00:02:53,031 --> 00:02:54,961
easy and testable by default

47
00:02:55,961 --> 00:02:57,858
I like to compare the approaches.

48
00:02:57,956 --> 00:02:59,412
First is the default.

49
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:01,842
It is the path where
most teams start with.

50
00:03:02,285 --> 00:03:05,907
Ask the AI to build a feature
and review whatever comes back.

51
00:03:06,351 --> 00:03:10,195
The second adds more explicit
instructions to the prompt.

52
00:03:10,229 --> 00:03:14,605
For example, use semantic HTML,
support keyboard validation,

53
00:03:14,662 --> 00:03:16,190
and use our design system.

54
00:03:16,986 --> 00:03:19,411
The third approach
changes the environment.

55
00:03:19,507 --> 00:03:22,896
Instead of hoping the model
remembers every standard, we

56
00:03:22,898 --> 00:03:27,506
make approved components, test
utilities, storybook examples, and

57
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:29,397
CI checks part of the architecture.

58
00:03:29,895 --> 00:03:32,029
That is the approach I want to focus on.

59
00:03:32,523 --> 00:03:35,040
These approaches are
not mutually exclusive.

60
00:03:35,077 --> 00:03:39,103
In practice, teams will still prompt
the model and still review the result.

61
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:42,455
The difference is where the
strongest guarantees comes up.

62
00:03:42,954 --> 00:03:44,792
Prompting can communicate intent.

63
00:03:44,878 --> 00:03:48,379
Architecture and tooling can make
important parts of the intent

64
00:03:48,669 --> 00:03:54,036
repeatable across developers,
repositories, and AI tools.

65
00:03:54,615 --> 00:03:59,066
The, the, the generate first
approach is attractive because it

66
00:03:59,095 --> 00:04:00,924
gives immediate visible progress.

67
00:04:01,276 --> 00:04:06,232
The risk is that the front-end quality
is often behavioral and not just visual.

68
00:04:06,751 --> 00:04:09,612
A dialog may render
correctly but lose focus.

69
00:04:09,685 --> 00:04:13,167
A validation message may be
visible but never announced.

70
00:04:13,524 --> 00:04:17,277
A custom control may duplicate
something the design system

71
00:04:17,277 --> 00:04:18,611
may already would have solved.

72
00:04:18,968 --> 00:04:22,516
So the cost does not disappear,
it moves downstream into a

73
00:04:22,535 --> 00:04:26,672
review like QA, accessibility
testing, or even production fixes.

74
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:31,709
The faster generation becomes,
the most expensive that downstream

75
00:04:31,716 --> 00:04:33,417
review model can become.

76
00:04:34,431 --> 00:04:38,656
It is especially important in
front-end systems because the same

77
00:04:38,722 --> 00:04:43,098
feature contains several layers at
once: visual structure, interaction

78
00:04:43,098 --> 00:04:47,620
state, browser behavior, accessibility
semantics, application state, and

79
00:04:47,655 --> 00:04:50,056
integration with shared components.

80
00:04:50,276 --> 00:04:55,189
A generated solution can be correct in
one layer and still be weak in other

81
00:04:55,189 --> 00:04:58,513
layers Prompting absolutely helps.

82
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:04,010
It gives the model clear standards
and better than giving none of them.

83
00:05:04,468 --> 00:05:08,650
But a prompt is still a request,
not an enforcement mechanism.

84
00:05:09,107 --> 00:05:11,155
It also creates a scaling problem.

85
00:05:11,254 --> 00:05:15,393
Every developer has to remember the
right wording, every agent needs to

86
00:05:15,393 --> 00:05:19,703
have the right context, and every
important standard must be repeated.

87
00:05:20,092 --> 00:05:25,475
If a rule matters enough that we expect
every implementation to follow it, that is

88
00:05:25,477 --> 00:05:29,982
a sign we should consider moving the rule
out of the prompt and into the platform.

89
00:05:30,674 --> 00:05:31,881
Prompts also age.

90
00:05:31,924 --> 00:05:35,251
As your design system changes,
testing conventions evolve, or

91
00:05:35,271 --> 00:05:38,991
any new accessibility requirement
comes in, old prompt templates

92
00:05:39,027 --> 00:05:40,503
may silently become stale.

93
00:05:41,083 --> 00:05:45,616
Platform-level rules have an advantage
because teams can update the shared

94
00:05:45,616 --> 00:05:50,734
component or check once and let,
any other future implementation

95
00:05:50,748 --> 00:05:52,078
benefit from this change.

96
00:05:53,078 --> 00:05:57,412
Moving on, architecture-level
guardrails narrow the solution scope.

97
00:05:58,434 --> 00:06:02,462
Instead of telling the model, "Please
build an accessible model," we give it

98
00:06:02,491 --> 00:06:07,101
a modal component that already handles
focus management and keyboard behavior.

99
00:06:07,602 --> 00:06:11,810
Instead of asking it to invent
a test strategy, we provide

100
00:06:11,835 --> 00:06:14,453
reusable pla-Playwright patterns.

101
00:06:14,741 --> 00:06:18,005
Instead of hoping someone
runs accessibility checks,

102
00:06:18,008 --> 00:06:19,904
CI runs them automatically.

103
00:06:20,369 --> 00:06:22,775
This does not remove engineering judgment.

104
00:06:23,029 --> 00:06:27,698
It changes where we spend it, from
repeatedly rediscovering basic

105
00:06:27,698 --> 00:06:31,876
standards to designing and improving
the platform that encodes them.

106
00:06:32,414 --> 00:06:37,268
A useful test is to ask, if a
requirement is stable, repeatable,

107
00:06:37,363 --> 00:06:42,109
and important enough, then we would
reject a pull request for violating it.

108
00:06:42,556 --> 00:06:45,556
Can some part of those
requirement be encoded?

109
00:06:45,919 --> 00:06:47,559
Not everything can be.

110
00:06:49,342 --> 00:06:53,722
But component choice, the required
test flows, prohibited patterns,

111
00:06:53,757 --> 00:06:56,998
and known accessibility rules
are often good candidates here

112
00:06:57,998 --> 00:07:01,301
Moving to this slide, I
think of this as a stack.

113
00:07:01,412 --> 00:07:03,440
Generation is only the first step.

114
00:07:03,691 --> 00:07:08,681
Composition should steer the
code toward approved components.

115
00:07:09,058 --> 00:07:13,080
Storybook gives us an isolated
place to inspect states.

116
00:07:13,433 --> 00:07:15,563
Playwright exercises real flows.

117
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:20,647
Axe Core catches a useful set of
automated accessibility issues, and CI

118
00:07:20,688 --> 00:07:22,659
turns those checks into a delivery rule.

119
00:07:23,306 --> 00:07:25,354
The platform goal is fast feedback.

120
00:07:25,445 --> 00:07:29,696
The closer the feedback, the easier
it is to fix and easier it is for both

121
00:07:29,748 --> 00:07:31,809
humans and coding agents to learn from.

122
00:07:32,611 --> 00:07:35,429
Notice that no single
layer has to be perfect.

123
00:07:35,734 --> 00:07:37,567
The strength comes from defense in depth.

124
00:07:38,115 --> 00:07:40,982
A design system component
prevents some error.

125
00:07:41,123 --> 00:07:43,667
Storybook exposes important state.

126
00:07:43,724 --> 00:07:46,419
Behavioral test catches
interaction regressions.

127
00:07:46,902 --> 00:07:50,597
A automated scan for
accessibility catches violations.

128
00:07:50,921 --> 00:07:54,304
So this makes the overall system
a lot stronger before delivery

129
00:07:55,304 --> 00:07:56,511
Moving to the next slide.

130
00:07:56,658 --> 00:08:00,484
A mature design system is
more than a visual library.

131
00:08:00,677 --> 00:08:03,646
It is a policy engine
expressed as a reusable code.

132
00:08:03,975 --> 00:08:08,268
If a dialog component already solves
focus management, escape behavior,

133
00:08:08,696 --> 00:08:12,318
accessible naming, and visual
consistency, every developer and

134
00:08:12,328 --> 00:08:14,496
every AI assistant should start there.

135
00:08:15,073 --> 00:08:18,170
That shifts the generation
task from inventing behavior

136
00:08:18,210 --> 00:08:19,748
to composing approved behavior.

137
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:24,729
If one of the strongest ways to reduce
variation without slowing things down.

138
00:08:25,354 --> 00:08:28,502
It is also why component APIs matter.

139
00:08:28,962 --> 00:08:33,896
A component that is technically accessible
but difficult to use correctly will

140
00:08:33,924 --> 00:08:36,039
still produce inconsistent result.

141
00:08:36,584 --> 00:08:41,590
Good platform components should make the
common case simple, expose safe defaults,

142
00:08:41,857 --> 00:08:47,578
and require explicit de- decisions only
when product context genuinely differs.

143
00:08:48,578 --> 00:08:52,636
Storybook is useful because many
front-end failures live in states

144
00:08:52,651 --> 00:08:57,025
that are easy to forget: errors,
loading, empty content, keyboard

145
00:08:57,025 --> 00:08:59,079
focus, or alternate responsive layout.

146
00:08:59,602 --> 00:09:04,490
If those states exist as stories, they
become part of the component contract.

147
00:09:04,938 --> 00:09:08,965
That helps reviewers, but
it also helps AI workflow.

148
00:09:09,055 --> 00:09:14,355
Instead of asking a coding in-- assistant
to infer all the expected states from

149
00:09:14,355 --> 00:09:19,593
scratch, the platform can expose examples
that show what correct would look like.

150
00:09:20,058 --> 00:09:25,081
For AI-assisted development, stories
can also improve context quality.

151
00:09:25,417 --> 00:09:29,799
A model can give only a TypeScript
definitions may understand what

152
00:09:29,937 --> 00:09:34,857
props exist, but not how the
component is normally composed.

153
00:09:35,728 --> 00:09:39,997
Examples show realistic usage,
expected state, and boundaries.

154
00:09:40,271 --> 00:09:44,510
That makes the design system easier
for both humans and machines to consume

155
00:09:45,510 --> 00:09:49,937
For front end guardrails, Playwright
and Axe Core complement each other.

156
00:09:50,357 --> 00:09:53,559
Playwright can verify the
flow a user actually takes.

157
00:09:53,742 --> 00:09:58,680
Can I tab through this form, open a
dialog, close it with an escape, and

158
00:09:58,681 --> 00:10:01,024
get my focus back where it belongs?

159
00:10:01,484 --> 00:10:05,034
Axe Core catches a different set
of structure and rule-based issues.

160
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:10,146
The key is to avoid treating an
automated accessibility scan as proof

161
00:10:10,185 --> 00:10:12,091
that the feature is fully accessible.

162
00:10:12,793 --> 00:10:14,442
It is one layer of evidence.

163
00:10:14,510 --> 00:10:19,220
Manual testing and assistive technology
testing still matters for behavior

164
00:10:19,227 --> 00:10:20,788
that automation cannot judge well.

165
00:10:21,353 --> 00:10:24,862
I also refer tests that describe
user outcome rather than

166
00:10:24,863 --> 00:10:26,745
implementation design details.

167
00:10:26,919 --> 00:10:32,362
For example, test that an error is
announced and focus moves correctly rather

168
00:10:32,364 --> 00:10:37,447
than testing a specific DOM structure
unless the structure itself is a contract.

169
00:10:37,853 --> 00:10:42,249
That gives the implementation room
to evolve while keeping that behavior

170
00:10:42,253 --> 00:10:47,670
stable CI is where the platform turns
standard into a delivery contract.

171
00:10:48,149 --> 00:10:50,925
A checklist says what we hope happens.

172
00:10:51,003 --> 00:10:55,515
A CI gate says what must happen
before we make the code move forward.

173
00:10:56,098 --> 00:10:58,817
This is also an important
learning loop here.

174
00:10:59,108 --> 00:11:04,602
If a bug escapes and we can identify a
reusable pattern behind it, we should ask

175
00:11:04,617 --> 00:11:09,480
whether that lesson can become a component
improvement, a lint rule, a Playwright

176
00:11:09,506 --> 00:11:11,966
test, or any other automated check.

177
00:11:12,353 --> 00:11:16,655
This is how the platform gets stronger
over the time, instead of relying on the

178
00:11:16,656 --> 00:11:19,535
same humans to remember the same lesson.

179
00:11:20,180 --> 00:11:22,537
The feedback needs to
be actionable as well.

180
00:11:22,662 --> 00:11:26,608
A failing gate that says accessibility
check failed creates friction.

181
00:11:26,952 --> 00:11:34,103
A good be-- a good developer pl- platform
points to vi-- to the violated rule, the

182
00:11:34,108 --> 00:11:37,280
affected element, and adds a test for it.

183
00:11:37,607 --> 00:11:42,757
Fast specific feedback is what makes the
guardrails from feeling like bureaucracy.

184
00:11:43,757 --> 00:11:46,001
So this is how I summarize the trade-off.

185
00:11:46,091 --> 00:11:50,990
Architecture level guardrails require
more upfront platform investment.

186
00:11:51,353 --> 00:11:53,955
Someone has to guide and
maintain the design system,

187
00:11:53,957 --> 00:11:58,338
test qu- utilities, stories, CI
configuration, and feedback loop.

188
00:11:58,679 --> 00:12:00,533
But the investment is reusable.

189
00:12:00,734 --> 00:12:05,127
It reduces how much quality depends
on the exact prompt and reduces how

190
00:12:05,169 --> 00:12:08,693
often reviewers need to rediscover
the same classes or issues.

191
00:12:09,220 --> 00:12:13,344
The important caveat is that a
qualitative model, not a claim that

192
00:12:13,351 --> 00:12:16,442
guardrails may make defects disappear.

193
00:12:16,757 --> 00:12:18,145
The trade-off is important.

194
00:12:18,478 --> 00:12:20,497
Architectural guardrails are not free.

195
00:12:20,822 --> 00:12:25,355
They move effort from repeated fe-feature
level cleanup into shared platform work.

196
00:12:25,706 --> 00:12:29,643
This is usually more valuable for
standards and that recur, that

197
00:12:29,684 --> 00:12:33,746
recur across many teams or features.

198
00:12:34,452 --> 00:12:37,628
For a one-off experimental
interface, the balance may be

199
00:12:37,629 --> 00:12:41,332
different, so the team should be
deliberate about what they encode

200
00:12:42,332 --> 00:12:45,589
Guardrails are not a
s-substitute for judgment.

201
00:12:45,807 --> 00:12:47,557
Accessibility is a good example.

202
00:12:47,896 --> 00:12:51,301
Automated tools can tell us that
a control is missing a name.

203
00:12:51,684 --> 00:12:56,544
They cannot always tell us whether the
name is the right thing, whether the

204
00:12:56,544 --> 00:13:00,828
interaction makes sense, or whether
a screen reader user can complete

205
00:13:00,828 --> 00:13:05,790
the task efficiently If you want
to try this without rebuilding your

206
00:13:05,791 --> 00:13:09,575
developer platform, start with a--
with the failures you already see.

207
00:13:09,906 --> 00:13:14,817
Look at the recurring PR comments,
accessibility bugs, flaky tests,

208
00:13:14,834 --> 00:13:19,236
or places where teams repeatedly
invent the same UI pattern.

209
00:13:20,403 --> 00:13:24,374
The move-- Then move one stable
expectation into the platform.

210
00:13:24,484 --> 00:13:28,035
Maybe that means a better
component, a reusable plat--

211
00:13:28,249 --> 00:13:30,108
Playwright helper or a CI check.

212
00:13:30,483 --> 00:13:34,508
Over time, the platform becomes a
memory system for engineering lessons.

213
00:13:34,878 --> 00:13:38,842
That helps human developers today,
and it becomes even more valuable

214
00:13:38,928 --> 00:13:40,952
as AI-generated code increases.

215
00:13:41,674 --> 00:13:45,364
You do not need a AI-specific
platform program to begin.

216
00:13:45,780 --> 00:13:49,429
Most of these investments improve
ordinary front-end engineering too.

217
00:13:49,872 --> 00:13:54,250
The AI angle simply raises the
urgency because generation can

218
00:13:54,250 --> 00:13:56,089
multiply patterns much faster.

219
00:13:56,763 --> 00:14:01,845
A platform that can help ten
developers becomes more valuable

220
00:14:01,885 --> 00:14:04,866
when those developers are also
working with coding agents.

221
00:14:06,253 --> 00:14:07,659
I'll close with this idea.

222
00:14:07,772 --> 00:14:10,875
The goal is not to make
AI write perfect code.

223
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:14,233
We already know humans do not
write perfect code either.

224
00:14:14,288 --> 00:14:15,761
We help each other by reviewing.

225
00:14:16,175 --> 00:14:19,542
The goal here is to build a
platform where better code is

226
00:14:19,543 --> 00:14:21,711
the, the path of least resistance.

227
00:14:22,144 --> 00:14:25,729
If our standards live only in
documentation and reviewer memory,

228
00:14:25,962 --> 00:14:28,440
AI will amplify that inconsistency.

229
00:14:28,909 --> 00:14:33,101
If our standards live in reusable
components, executable tests, and

230
00:14:33,101 --> 00:14:36,815
delivery gates, AI can operate
inside the same engineering

231
00:14:36,817 --> 00:14:38,728
system we expect humans to use.

232
00:14:39,153 --> 00:14:42,322
The practical takeaway is
to think beyond the prompt.

233
00:14:42,723 --> 00:14:46,356
Prompts will help improving,
and models will keep improving.

234
00:14:46,649 --> 00:14:50,742
But teams still own the engineering
environment in which the generated

235
00:14:50,751 --> 00:14:53,066
code is accepted, tested, and shipped.

236
00:14:53,543 --> 00:14:57,264
That environment is where we
create the most durable guardrails.

237
00:14:57,629 --> 00:14:59,030
Thank you so much for listening.
